WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
The next module moves from joins to subqueries.

00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:06.367
A join combines row sets directly.

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:11.917
A subquery allows one query to use the result of another query as part of its logic.

00:00:11.917 --> 00:00:17.702
You will use subqueries for questions involving membership, existence, comparisons,

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:25.037
and supporting calculations. For example, you may ask which patients have a value above an overall average,

00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:31.887
which customers have at least one qualifying order, or which products have never appeared in a sales table.

00:00:31.887 --> 00:00:35.022
The join skills from this module still matter.

00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:42.562
When deciding between a join, a subquery, or a common table expression, continue asking about grain,

00:00:42.562 --> 00:00:45.007
cardinality, preservation, and clarity.

00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:51.417
Before moving on, make sure you can explain the difference between matching and preservation and why several

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.842
detail tables can multiply rows.

00:00:54.842 --> 00:00:58.502
Those ideas will continue to appear in more advanced SQL.

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:04.072
As SQL becomes more expressive, the goal is not to use the most advanced syntax.

00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:09.475
The goal is to write logic that is correct, understandable, and easy to validate.
